{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/rem-braiding-unit-circle-convention","@type":["folio-assistant-core:Remark","doco:Section"],"label":"rem:braiding-unit-circle-convention","kind":"remark","title":"The unit-circle convention for the braiding eigenvalue","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-braiding-eigenvalue-extraction"],"interprets":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-braiding-eigenvalue-extraction","tags":["braiding","eigenvalue","convention","substrate-parameter","unit-circle"],"text":"braiding-unit-circle-convention.md","provenance":"authored","html":"<a id=\"rem:braiding-unit-circle-convention\"></a><p>The inside-unit-circle convention assigns <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q := \\lambda_+</code>, where <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\lambda_+</code> is the eigenvalue of <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">c_{V,V}</code> on the symmetric summand <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">S^2 V</code>. For real <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q > 1</code>, <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\lambda_+ = q</code> lies outside the unit circle and <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\lambda_- = -q^{-1}</code> lies inside. This convention secures positivity for the symmetric eigenvalue and aligns with the standard orientation of the Kauffman bracket.</p>\n","source":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/quantum-observable-universes/braiding-unit-circle-convention.md","heading":"Remark","number":"3.24.1"}
